00:00:00.320 --> 00:00:01.990 align:start position:0%
 
i'm jennifer neville and i'll do the  overview for our session in the  afternoon which we loosely called  clustering so we have these four great  speakers uh  peter bickle here from uc berkeley  andrea montanari from  stanford karl rove from university of  wisconsin madison and vahab murachny  who's from google who is asking the  google type questions this morning so i  have a little bit more formality than  michael for the overview  but i should warn you i'm not a  clustering  person  so  what i thought would be good to do would  be to give you  just a general sense of the dimensions  on which the people that are working on  clustering and networks how they're the  kinds of questions that they ask and how  they formulate the modeling and the  algorithmic approaches  to their problem  how they differ so that you have some  context to think about the talks that  we'll be we'll be having this afternoon  and hopefully that will facilitate some  questions that we can have afterwards  so  i originally thought i'd give you some  sort of basic introduction to  clustering work but we already heard  about some earlier uh in the day and  there's some introductions in the talks  this afternoon so i'll just say there's  a large body of work a lot of people  working on identifying communities or  clusters or groups and networks which  ranges from people that work on spectral  clustering we heard about modularity  maximization and mark newman's  work this morning and also stochastic  block models and of course there's a lot  more  and here are  from looking over the talks that are  going to be given after i finish  speaking uh here are some dimensions on  which people differ in terms of how they  approach this clustering problem  whether they're from the statistics  community or the computer science  machine learning community or whether  they're focused on scaling  to very large data sets  or if they're more theoretical computer  scientists so first and foremost how  people formulate their problem  definition as is maybe the  first  way that they sort of frame how they're  going to approach this problem of  clustering and obviously how to define  what is a cluster is a hard problem that  exists not even you know  in the networks community but in the  people that look at data at large  so then  certain  types of work differ based on the kind  of data assumptions they make about the  underlying model of the data and we  talked about that a little bit this  morning  there's modeling assumptions that people  make  to  that may be different from these data  assumptions based on how they're going  to  algorithmically estimate their models  how the models are evaluated or  in terms of accuracy or the properties  of the models that there's that's  another dimension and finally what  people care about in terms of scaling or  asymptotics that differs as well  so  just a few quick comments on each of  these dimensions  today  in the talks we're going to hear both  about  people that look for global clusterings  of the entire network  into  either distinct partitions so we want to  have separate groups of the nodes where  each node only belongs to a single group  or we might want overlapping clusters  where nodes can belong to more than one  group and then we're also going to hear  several talks that focus at least  somewhat on local clustering because if  you think about globe clustering all of  the data into groups that might not be  feasible if you have a really large  data set maybe the  first of all  in terms of algorithmically you might  not want to spend enough time to cluster  a graph with a billion vertices but also  the groups might not make sense if you  consider the entire entire structure  so  in terms of the data assumptions  i guess because i'm a person that works  more on predictive modeling i always  think about learning a model that's  going to generalize to some new data  that we haven't seen before but the  interesting thing about clustering  particularly in networks is that there  it's really more of a descriptive task  where we want to model the data that we  have and describe the data that we see  and so we don't necessarily care about  generalizing to  new data that we haven't seen  but we  want to talk about the properties of the  algorithms or the types of clusters we  see under some assumptions about what  the data  have actually been generated from and so  this relates to kind the topics that we  talked about earlier  in the day because we might think about  analyzing clustering methods by assuming  that the data was generated from some  underlying parametric model and then if  that actually holds what can we say  about the kinds of things that our  algorithms were produced  other times we try to make fewer  assumptions about the data  but we might give some kind of bounds on  the performance of the algorithms  where some part of the bound includes  some characteristic of the graph for  example the conductance of the graph  in terms of  modeling assumptions first  if  people approach this from a more  parametric model based  framework then how what assumptions we  make about the characteristics of the  models might influence the  performance of the algorithm and we  heard a little bit of talk about that  this morning  in the difference between considering  smooth functions versus  non-smooth functions for these bernoulli  random variables that would generate the  edges  the dimension on which this the  the talks we're going to hear today  mostly differ  i think  really can be sort of ground out in our  choice of the objective function that we  maximize in our algorithms and so some  examples of objective functions are the  modularity function that was mentioned  earlier we could talk about cuts or  conductance but also the likelihood of  the data and the choice of the objective  function is often sort of intimately  tied to our problem definition because  if we say  that we want to have groups of nodes  such that they have a lot of links  inside their clusters and few links  outside their clusters then naturally  the way that we could formulate that  quantitatively is by considering the  cuts between the clusters  okay  and then in terms of evaluation  uh there's two  major uh dimensions that i saw in  people's talks for today and one talks  more about the correctness or  consistency of the algorithms so that in  some cases with some assumptions about  the data we want to guarantee that the  algorithm is going to find and return  the correct clusters to us  or maybe to have some sort of bound on  the approximation  quality of the output clusters  under the assumption that there exists a  cluster in the data okay and so that's  more on the properties of the kinds of  output clusters that we get but also of  concern is really the efficiency of the  algorithms and so we might want to have  some kind of guarantees that the  algorithm is going to scale linearly or  log linearly with the size of the data  or we'll hear with some of the people  that talk about local clustering maybe  to have a more efficient algorithm we  want to talk about having that scale  linearly in the size of the clusters  local clusters that we find rather than  the size of the overall graph and  something that i think is a sort of  tricky subject with respect to  clustering and i didn't see a lot in  people's slides  when i was looking through them for  today so hopefully maybe we can talk  about this more in the discussion but  really  there's not a lot of work on  validation of these clusters  in terms of whether they mean  what we hope they should mean about the  the data so are the groups or  communities are they meaningful we tend  to have the circular kind of argument  that we sort of formulate the problem  quantitatively  saying we want lots of links inside the  cluster a few links outside the cluster  then we frame that in a quantitative  objective function then we apply our  algorithm and then we measure how well  did we match that objective function and  so we have sort of a circular type of  evaluation normally and really  practitioners i think really would care  more about are these clusters meaningful  and are these communities sort of  important things in the data that we  have particularly when they reply  apply to people or social networks  okay and so the last dimension was  really  scaling or asymptotics i think there's  an interesting difference between the  algorithmic computer scientists that  talk about as asymptotics in terms of  run time and how fast the algorithm is  going to grow  and these statisticians that think more  about asymptotics in terms of thinking  about the estimation behavior of the  algorithm and so i'd like to see a  little bit more discussion about you  know are these orthogonal to each other  or  are there interactions between these two  typically  i myself if i have my statistician hat  on i say i don't really care about the  scaling to the really large graphs  because i really want to understand the  properties of how the statistical  estimation works and then later on once  we understand it we'll figure out how to  make it linear or log linear so it'll  scale to really large graphs  so that's all i have to say about  the overview i would say that the talks  will here will highlight through  different view different  frames of reference in terms of these  perspectives and then we can sort of  consider this further as we talk about  it in the discussion afterwards so i  will stop there and pass it on to peter  bickle who will be our first  talk

